## 2. Finding 1 — the pre-existing array-root red: CONFIRMED

*Verified in source.* The test (`tests/ts/array-root.test.ts`, unmodified vs HEAD, md5
`e594de0bd65809c4d70f2663823f65a9`):

```
L18:    expect(read("reference/swift/gen/boring/ArrayRootStateOps.swift")).toContain(
L19:      "static let readOnlyInts: [Int32] = Array(TiqianArray([10, 20, 30]))",
L20:    );
```

The committed reference (`reference/swift/gen/boring/ArrayRootStateOps.swift`, unmodified vs
HEAD, md5 `1de81f1650646f53e35cf58e64833d23`):

```
L4:     public static let readOnlyInts: ReadOnlyArray<Int32> = ReadOnlyArray(TiqianArray<Int32>([10, 20, 30]))
```

The expected substring is absent: `grep -Fc` returns `0`, and a direct string containment
check returns `False`. `ReadOnlyArray<Int32>` cannot contain the literal `[Int32]`, and the
committed line is not the old `Array(TiqianArray([...]))` form. The report quotes the actual
`Received:` value verbatim and it matches L4 character-for-character, including
`ReadOnlyArray(TiqianArray<Int32>(...))`.

**Failure order confirmed.** `control-base-array-root.log:16` records
`at <anonymous> (/tmp/p1run/base/tests/ts/array-root.test.ts:18:72)` — the first failing
assertion is L18, so L25–L30 (the Rust block) are never evaluated. The control ran the
**unpatched** `base` copy (path in the log confirms it), and the earlier assertions L10–L16
(TS, Kotlin) passed first, so L18 really is the first red. This is *log states*, but
consistent with the source I read.

**The patch does not touch that line.** *Verified in source*: the only array-root hunk in
`COMPOSITE.diff` is

```
@@ -25,7 +25,7 @@
-    expect(rust).toContain("LazyLock<Vec<String>>");
+    expect(rust).toContain("LazyLock<Vec<UString>>");
```

i.e. changed file-line 28, not 18. The report's statement is exact on this point.

**Configuration-independence confirmed.** *Verified in source*:
`git diff --stat HEAD -- tests/ts/array-root.test.ts reference/swift/gen/boring/ArrayRootStateOps.swift`
is empty at HEAD `0a5c42a702d938ca4da39cbcefae2cc450e021fe`, and neither file appears in the
21 uncommitted entries, so the L18/L4 mismatch exists at HEAD and under every patch
combination. *Verified in source* additionally: the committed Rust reference does contain
`LazyLock<Vec<UString>>` (`array_root_state_ops.rs:9`) and not the old `<String>` form, so
P1's L28 change would pass **if reached** — and the run is right that it is not reached.

**One imprecision in the report's explanation.** §5 ends "The prior report's substring
spot-checks covered only the lines P1 *changes*, which is how this one was missed." The
earlier report in fact *did* spot-check this very file's P1 line (`patch-independence/REPORT.md`
§4: "`array_root_state_ops.rs` has `LazyLock<Vec<UString>>`, old absent"). The real cause of
the miss is that the earlier check only ever compared *P1's changed lines* against the
reference and **never executed the test**, so a red on an unchanged line ahead of the changed
one was invisible. The substance of the finding is unaffected; the diagnosis is off by one
step.

## 3. Finding 2 — nine environment timeouts: CONFIRMED in substance, IMPRECISE in support

*Verified in source* (budgets) and *log states* (observations).

Every non-assertion failure in `cfg-a-bun.log` and `cfg-b-bun.log` is reported as
`^ this test timed out after Nms`, and each N matches the third argument (or its absence) in
the test source:

| failing test | log | source |
|---|---|---|
| `small array initializer mutation rules retain all three diagnostics` (array-root) | 5000 ms | `array-root.test.ts:64` test, no third arg → bun default |
| `a capturing static initializer reports the ruled Rust error` (fn-values) | 5000 ms | `fn-values.test.ts:90`, no third arg |
| `@:sealed on a class reports the interface-only error` (sealed) | 5000 ms | `sealed-variants.test.ts:101`, no third arg |
| `self-construction with arguments keeps the static initializer error` (sealed) | 5000 ms | `sealed-variants.test.ts:147`, no third arg |
| `the sanctioned self-construction static uses each target lane` (sealed) | 15000 ms | `sealed-variants.test.ts:212` `}, 15000);` |
| `static initializer mutation is rejected with the sanctioned error` (static-state) | 15000 ms | `static-state.test.ts:66` `}, 15000);` |
| `marker and explicit-member mutations affect the four emitted members` (printed-record) | 120000 ms | `printed-record.test.ts:226` `}, 120_000);` |
| `nullable operands are rejected on every target` (std-string) | 120000 ms | `std-string.test.ts:98` `}, 120000);` |
| `rejects each invalid marker shape with the ruled error on every target` (value-type) | 120000 ms | `value-type.test.ts:149` `}, 120_000);` |

**Measured per-run cost: confirmed.** From the run's own logs, passing tests that spawn exactly
one haxe subprocess: `cfg-a` 23440.59 / 23511.59 / 28899.50 ms; `cfg-b` 22852.57 / 21802.59 /
27725.47 ms → **21.80–28.90 s**, exactly the range the report states. So the nine timed-out
tests each need between one and fifteen ~23 s subprocesses against 5/15/120 s budgets. The
attribution is arithmetically sound.

**"Not a mismatch of a patch-changed expectation": confirmed as far as it was observed.** An
